<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/3rd-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">War of the Eight Princes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">291–<a href="/years/306">306</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Rebellion of the Eight Kings · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The War of the Eight Princes was a series of civil wars among princes of the Sima family of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-jin-dynasty">Western Jin dynasty</a>, fought from 291 to 306 for control of the mentally incapable Emperor Hui. Triggered by Empress Jia's intrigues, it devastated northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and exhausted the dynasty, enabling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> and other peoples, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-yuan">Liu Yuan</a> of Han-Zhao, to rise against Jin.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-yi">Sima Yi</a> seized control of the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a> in 249, and in February 266 his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-jin">Sima Yan</a>, Emperor Wu of Jin, forced the last Wei emperor to abdicate and founded the Jin dynasty. In late 289 Emperor Wu fell gravely ill, and his father-in-law Yang Jun hid the decree naming him and Sima Liang, Prince of Ru’nan, as co-regents, while the empress Yang Zhi issued another making her father sole regent.</p><p>The name of the conflict comes from the biographies of eight Sima princes collected in Chapter 59 of the Book of Jin. It was not one continuous war but intervals of peace broken by short and intense periods of fighting.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Empress Jia, wife of the new Emperor Hui, enlisted Sima Liang and Sima Wei, Prince of Chu, and on the night of 23 April 291 had Yang Jun overthrown. Wei, armed with a forged edict, then had Liang executed, and the empress turned her troops on Wei, who was captured and executed in turn. For nine years she ruled in the emperor’s name, until in 300 she had the Crown Prince, Sima Yu, who was not her son, arrested. Sima Lun, Prince of Zhao, then took the regency and usurped the throne.</p><p>A coalition of three princes, Sima Jiong, Sima Ying and Sima Yong, with some 200,000 troops, overthrew him, and Jiong became regent on 11 August 301. Further rivalries brought fighting in and around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, where Sima Ai, Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changsha">Changsha</a>, held the capital until 303 against armies sent by Yong and Ying. Ying then made himself Crown Prince in May 304, and Sima Yue, a second cousin of Emperor Wu, fought him with allies who brought many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> and Wuhuan soldiers. In 305 Yue led the eastern governors against Yong, who held the emperor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang’an</a>, and took control of the court. Ying was caught at Ye, and Yong was strangled in his carriage at Xin’an.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The wars devastated the Jin heartland in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and opened the empire to rebellion, above all by tribal peoples who had resettled in the Central Plains. Yue’s victory was short-lived. Emperor Hui died on 8 January 307 after eating poisoned bread. Ji Sang and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-le">Shi Le</a> sacked Ye in 307, killing Sima Teng, and Wang Mi ravaged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china-plain">North China Plain</a> and laid siege to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> in 308.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-le">Shi Le</a> and Wang Mi then joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-yuan">Liu Yuan</a>’s state of Han, and together they overran most of the lands north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>, while in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> the Jin failed to destroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-xiong">Li Xiong</a>’s state of Cheng. The war ushered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-kingdoms">Sixteen Kingdoms</a> era, more than a century of warfare between short-lived dynasties in the north and the Eastern Jin dynasty in the south.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “War of the Eight Princes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the <b>War of the Eight Princes</b> (291–306) wrecked the Western Jin, the Xiongnu-led Han Zhao state sacked Luoyang in 311, capturing Emperor Huai, and took Chang'an in 316, capturing Emperor Min.</blockquote><a href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sui reunified China proper, closing the Northern and Southern dynasties era and the division that had lasted since the <b>War of the Eight Princes</b>, and in a short reign laid much of the groundwork for the Tang, which toppled them and then presided over a golden age.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sui-dynasty">Sui Dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Liu Yuan, a descendant of the Southern Xiongnu shanyus educated in the Chinese classics, rebelled during the <b>War of the Eight Princes</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/han-zhao">Han Zhao</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Emperor Hui, who was mentally incapable, Empress Jia's intrigues triggered the <b>War of the Eight Princes</b> from 291 to 306.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-jin">Western Jin</a></li></ul></section>
